          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            A
            %em von Neumann algebra
            or
            %em
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi W
                      %mo *
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} W^*
              \-algebra
            is an important and special kind of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/operator+algebra"} operator algebra
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/measure+theory"} measure theory
            and
            = succeed "/" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+mechanics"} quantum mechanics
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+field+theory"} quantum field theory
            in its algebraic formulation as
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/AQFT"} AQFT
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Isbell+duality"} formal duals
            of (
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/non-commutative+geometry"}> non-commutative
            )
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/localizable+measurable+spaces"} localizable measurable spaces
            (or
            = succeed ");" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/measurable+locales"} measurable locales
            %em
              %a{:href => "#RelationToMeasureSpaces"} Relation to measurable spaces
            below.
          %h2#history_and_terminology History and terminology
          %p Since terminology varies in the literature, we will say something about this first. There are no precise definitions here; see below for those.
          %p
            (Of course,
            %em
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi W
                      %mo *
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} W^*
              \-algebras
            should not be confused with
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/W-algebras"} W-algebras
            in (logarithmic)
            = succeed ".)" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/conformal+field+theory"} conformal field theory
          %p
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/John+von+Neumann"} John von Neumann
            originally studied certain
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/operator+algebras"} operator algebras
            (back then they were called
            = succeed ")," do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/rings"} rings
                of
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/linear+operator"} operators
            = succeed "-subalgebras" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo *
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} *
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi B
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi H
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B(H)
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/bounded+operators"} bounded operators
            on some
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hilbert+space"} Hilbert space
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi H
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} H
            that are
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/closed+subspace"} closed
            in any of the several
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/operator+topology"} operator topologies
            on
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi B
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi H
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B(H)
            (except for the norm topology, which gives
            = succeed "-" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi C
                      %mo *
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C^*
            = succeed ");" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/C-star-algebra"} algebras
          %p
            One disadvantage of such a definition is that it makes it difficult to separate properties of von Neumann algebras from properties of their
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/representations"} representations
            on Hilbert spaces. For example, all faithful representations induce the same ultraweak topology on a von Neumann algebra, but different representations induce different weak topologies. Furthermore, not all von Neumann algebras come automatically equipped with a representation on a Hilbert space, such as the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/coproduct"} coproduct
            of two von Neumann algebras (although such a representation can always be constructed). Finally, this definition unnecessarily confuses two very distinct notions: algebras and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/modules"} modules
            (or representations).
          %p
            Therefore, we may use the modern abstract terminology in which a von Neumann algebra is defined as an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/associative+algebra"} algebra
            with certain
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/extra+structure"} structures
            and
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/extra+property"} properties
            %em representations
            of algebras.
          %p
            In the old terminology, morphisms of representations of von Neumann algebras (von Neumann algebras in the historical concrete sense) are sometimes called
            %em spatial morphisms of von Neumann algebras
            (as opposed to the
            %em abstract morphisms
            that we will define below). Similarly, the concrete von Neumann algebras themselves are sometimes called
            = succeed "," do
              %em von Neumann algebras
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mi W
                        %mo *
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} W^*
                \-algebras
            = succeed "-algebras," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi C
                      %mo *
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C^*
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/concrete+and+abstract+structures"} concrete and abstract structures
            such as
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/manifolds"} manifolds
          %p
            The
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/nPOV"} nPOV
            dictates that a clear distinction between the categories of algebras and modules must be maintained, in particular, modules should not be confused with algebras. Hence we stick to the modern terminology, which also seems to be preferred in new papers on von Neumann algebras, see for example
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/1110.5671"} arXiv:1110.5671v1
          %h2#definitions Definitions
          %p For completeness, we give both the modern abstract and historical concrete definitions.
