            on
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi H
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} H
            is a unital
            = succeed "-subalgebra" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo *
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} *
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi B
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi H
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B(H)
            that is
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/closed+subspace"} closed
            in the
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/weak+operator+topology"} weak operator topology
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ultraweak+topology"} ultraweak topology
            or in the
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/strong+topology"} strong topology
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/closed+subspace"} closed
            in the
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/norm+topology"} norm topology
            = succeed "-" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi C
                      %mo *
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C^*
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/C-star-algebra"} algebras
            on
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi H
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} H
            = succeed "-subalgebras" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo *
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} *
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi B
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi H
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B(H)
            closed under the norm topology.
          %p
            We equip a von Neumann algebra with the topology induced by its inclusion into
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi B
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi H
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B(H)
            equipped with the ultraweak topology. An
            %strong abstract morphism
            of von Neumann algebras can then be defined as a unital
            = succeed "-homomorphism" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo *
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} *
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi B
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi H
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B(H)
            and treating it as an algebra on its own.
          %p
            Alternatively, we can define a von Neumann algebra
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            as a unital
            = succeed "-algebra" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo *
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} *
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi B
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi H
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B(H)
            for some Hilbert space
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi H
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} H
            such that the image of the inclusion is closed in the ultraweak topology on
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi B
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi H
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B(H)
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            by the ultraweak topology on
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi B
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi H
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B(H)
            does not depend on the choice of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi H
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} H
            or the particular inclusion of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            into
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi B
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi H
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B(H)
            = succeed "-algebras" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo *
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} *
          %p
            It is a theorem that the category of (concrete) von Neumann algebras and abstract morphisms is
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equivalence+of+categories"} equivalent
            to the category of (abstract)
            = succeed "-algebras" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi W
                      %mo *
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} W^*
            = succeed "-homomorphisms." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi W
                      %mo *
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} W^*
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/representations"} representations
            of
            = succeed "-algebras" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi W
                      %mo *
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} W^*
            %strong spatial morphisms
            of concrete von Neumann algebras.
          %h2#preduals Sakai’s theorem and properties of preduals
          %p
            Sakai’s theorem states that preduals considered in the abstract definition are necessarily unique. More precisely, given a von Neumann algebra
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            we consider the category whose objects are pairs
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi V
                    %mo ,
                    %mi f
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (V,f)
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi V
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
            is a Banach space and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %msup
                    %mi V
                    %mo *
                  %mo →
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f\colon V^*\to A
            is an isomorphism of Banach spaces. A morphism from
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi V
                  %mo ,
                  %mi f
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (V,f)
            to
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi W
                  %mo ,
                  %mi g
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (W,g)
            is a morphism
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi h
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %mi V
                  %mo →
                  %mi W
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} h\colon V\to W
            of Banach spaces such that
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                    %msup
                      %mi h
                      %mo *
                    %mo =
                    %mi g
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f h^* = g
          %p
            Sakai’s theorem then states that in the above category there is exactly one morphism between any pair of objects, which is necessarily an isomorphism. In particular, the category of preduals is canonically isomorphic to the
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/terminal+category"} terminal category
          %p
            Sakai’s theorem can be extended to morphisms of von Neumann algebras. Thus preduals of von Neumann algebras and their morphisms are unique up to a unique isomorphism, in particular we can talk about
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/generalized+the"} the
            predual of a von Neumann algebra and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/generalized+the"} the
            predual of a morphism of von Neumann algebras.
          %p
            The weak topology induced on a von Neumann algebra by its predual is called the
            = succeed "." do
              %strong ultraweak topology
          %p
            Consider the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dual+vector+space"} dual space
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi V
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
            of a von Neumann algebra
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            equipped with the ultraweak topology. The
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+vector+space"} topological vector space
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi V
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
            canonically embeds into the dual of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            as a Banach space, the embedding map being induced by the canonical continuous map from
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            equipped with the norm topology to
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            equipped with the ultraweak topology. Thus
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi V
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
            is also a Banach space. There is a canonical morphism of Banach spaces from
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            to
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi V
                    %mo *
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V^*
            given by evaluating an element of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi V
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
            on the given element of
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi V
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
            is the predual of
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
          %h2#elementary_examples Elementary examples
          %p
            The easiest example of a von Neumann algebra is given by the
            = succeed "-algebra" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi C
                      %mo *
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C^*
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi B
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi H
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B(H)
            of bounded operators on a complex
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hilbert+space"} Hilbert space
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi H
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} H
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/trace+class+operator"} trace class operators
          %p
            Any
            = succeed "-subalgebra" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi C
                      %mo *
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C^*
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi B
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi H
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B(H)
            closed in the ultraweak topology is again a von Neumann algebra.
          %p
            Another example is
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi L
                    %mn ∞
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L^\infty(X)
            under pointwise almost everywhere multiplication, where
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            is a σ-finite
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/measure+space"} measure space
            or a
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/localizable+measurable+space"} localizable measurable space
            %em commutative
            von Neumann algebras, according to a specialized version of the
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Gelfand+duality+theorem"} Gelfand duality theorem
          %p
            A faithful representation (in fact, the standard from) of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi L
                    %mn ∞
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ↪
                  %mi B
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msup
                    %mi L
                    %mn 2
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L^\infty(X) \hookrightarrow B(L^2(X))
            is given by considering
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi L
                    %mn 2
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L^2(X)
            as an
            = succeed "-module" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi L
                      %mn ∞
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L^\infty(X)
          %h2#properties_of_morphisms_of_von_neumann_algebras Properties of morphisms of von Neumann algebras
          %h2#the_category_of_von_neumann_algebras The category of von Neumann algebras
          %p
            The category of von Neumann algebras is not a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/locally+presentable+category"} locally presentable category
            since its
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/small+objects"} small objects
            are precisely von Neumann algebras of dimension 0 and 1. See Theorem 4.2 in
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "#CK"} Chirvasitu–Ko
          %p
            The
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/forgetful+functor"} forgetful functor
            from von Neumann algebras to
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sets"} sets
            that sends a von Neumann algebra to its
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/unit+ball"} unit ball
            is a
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/right+adjoint+functor"} right adjoint functor
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monadic+functor"} monadic functor
            and preserves all
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sifted+colimits"} sifted colimits
          %p
            Thus,
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/limits"} limits
            and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sifted+colimits"} sifted colimits
            of von Neumann algebras can be computed on the level of underlying unit balls.
          %p
            Small
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/coproducts"} coproducts
            of von Neumann algebras exist. There is also a “reduced” version of small
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/coproducts"} coproducts
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/free+products"} free products
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spatial+tensor+product"} spatial tensor product
          %h2#monoidal_structures Monoidal structures
          %p
            There are two different
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/tensor+products"} tensor products
            one can define on von Neumann algebras.
          %p
            First, one can use the usual universal property of tensor products and postulate that morphisms
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                  %mo ⊗
                  %mi B
                  %mo →
                  %mi M
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A\otimes B\to M
            are in a natural bijection with pairs of morphisms
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                  %mo →
                  %mi M
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A\to M
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi B
                  %mo →
                  %mi M
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B\to M
            whose images commute in
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi M
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symmetric+monoidal+structure"} symmetric monoidal structure
            on von Neumann algebras. This monoidal structure is not
            = succeed "." do
              %span.newWikiWord
                closed
                %a{:href => "/nlab/new/closed"} ?
          %p
            Secondly, one can also define a “reduced” version, known as the
            = succeed "." do
              %em spatial tensor product
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            and
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi B
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                  %mo ⊗
                  %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A\otimes 1
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mn 1
                  %mo ⊗
                  %mi B
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 1\otimes B
            in the von Neumann algebra
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi B
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msup
                      %mi L
                      %mn 2
                    %mi A
                    %mo ⊗
                    %msup
                      %mi L
                      %mn 2
                    %mi B
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B(L^2 A\otimes L^2 B)
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi L
                    %mn 2
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L^2 A
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi L
                    %mn 2
                  %mi B
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L^2 B
            are the Haagerup standard form of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi B
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B
            respectively. This also results in a
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symmetric+monoidal+structure"} symmetric monoidal structure
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/opposite+category"} opposite category
            yields a
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/closed+monoidal+structure"} closed monoidal structure
          %h2#categories
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi W
                    %mo *
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} W^*
            \-categories
          %h2#modules_over_von_neumann_algebras Modules over von Neumann algebras
          %h2#bimodules_over_von_neumann_algebras_and_connes_fusion Bimodules over von Neumann algebras and Connes fusion
          %h2#modular_algebra_and_tomitatakesaki_theory Modular algebra and Tomita–Takesaki theory
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/modular+theory"} modular theory
          %h2#gelfand_duality_for_commutative_von_neumann_algebras Gelfand duality for commutative von Neumann algebras
          %p
            See the article
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/commutative+von+Neumann+algebra"} commutative von Neumann algebra
          %h2#relevance Relevance
          %p
            The
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Gelfand+duality+theorem"} Gelfand duality theorem
            states that there is a contravariant
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equivalence+of+categories"} equivalence
            between the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category"} category
            of commutative von Neumann algebras and the category of compact strictly localizable enhanced
            = succeed ";" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/measurable+space"} measurable spaces
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/opposite+category"} opposite category
            of one is equivalent to the other. See
            %a{:href => "#RelationToMeasureSpaces"} Relation to Measurable Spaces
            below. General von Neumann algebras are seen then as a ‘noncommutative’ measurable spaces in a sense analogous to
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/noncommutative+geometry"} noncommutative geometry
          %p
            The importance of von Neumann algebras for (
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/higher+category+theory"}> higher
            )
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category+theory"} category theory
            and topology lays in the evidence that von Neumann algebras are deeply connected with the low dimensional
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+field+theory"} quantum field theory
            (2d
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/CFT"} CFT
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/TQFT"} TQFT
            in low dimensions, inclusions of
            = succeed "s," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/von+Neumann+algebra+factor"} factor
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+group"} quantum group
            = succeed ";" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/knot+theory"} knot theory
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/elliptic+cohomology"} elliptic cohomology
          %p
            The highlights of their structure theory include the results on classification of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/von+Neumann+algebra+factor"} factors
            (
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Alain+Connes"}> Alain Connes
            , 1970s) and theory of inclusions of subfactors (V. Jones). (Hilbert)
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/bimodule"} bimodule
            = succeed ")." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Connes+fusion"} Connes fusion
          %ul
            %li
              Graeme Segal,
              = succeed "." do
                %em Elliptic cohomology (after Landweber-Stong, Ochanine, Witten, and others)
          %p and its update
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Graeme+Segal"}> Graeme Segal
              ,
              %em What is an elliptic object?
              Elliptic cohomology, 306–317, London Math. Soc. Lecture Note Ser., 342, Cambridge Univ. Press, Cambridge, 2007.
          %p
            on hypothetical connections between
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/CFT"} CFT
            and
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/elliptic+cohomology"} elliptic cohomology
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Stefan+Stolz"} Stefan Stolz
              and
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Peter+Teichner"} Peter Teichner
              %em What is an elliptic object?
              (
              %a{:href => "http://math.berkeley.edu/~teichner/Papers/Oxford.pdf"}> pdf
              )
          %p
            See also the
            %a{:href => "http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Von_Neumann_algebra"} Wikipedia entry
            entry for more on von Neumann algebras and a list of references and links.
          %h2#general General
          %p
            The
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/bicommutant+theorem"} bicommutant theorem
            (as known as the
            %em double commutant theorem
            , or
            %em von Neumann’s double commutant theorem
            ) is the following result.
          %p
            Let
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                  %mo ⊆
                  %mi B
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi H
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A \subseteq B(H)
            be a sub-
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/star-algebra"} star-algebra
            of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/C-star+algebra"} C-star algebra
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/bounded+linear+operator"} bounded linear operators
            on a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hilbert+space"} Hilbert space
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi H
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} H
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/von+Neumann+algebra"} von Neumann algebra
            on
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi H
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} H
            if and only if
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                    %mo =
                    %mi A
                    %mo ″
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A = A''
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                  %mo ′
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A'
            denotes the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/commutant"} commutant
            of
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
          %p
            Notice that the condition of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            being a von Neumann algebra (being closed in the
            = succeed ";" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/weak+operator+topology"} weak operator topology
            = succeed ")," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/operator+topology"} operator topology
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topology"} topological
            condition, is by this result equivalent to an algebraic condition (being equal to its bicommutant).
          %h2#RelationToMeasureSpaces Relation to measurable spaces
          %p
            The
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Gelfand-Naimark+theorem"} Gel'fand–Naimark theorem
            states that the category of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/localizable+measurable+spaces"} localizable measurable spaces
            is contravariantly
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equivalence+of+categories"} equivalent
            to (that is equivalent to the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/opposite+category"} opposite
            of) the category of commutative von Neumann algebras. As such, arbitrary von Neumann algebras may be interpreted as ‘noncommutative’ measurable spaces in a sense analogous to
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/noncommutative+geometry"} noncommutative geometry
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/noncommutative+probability+space"} noncommutative probability space
          %h2#topics_of_interest_for_the_understanding_of_aqft Topics of interest for the understanding of AQFT
          %p
            This paragraph will collect some facts of interest for the aspects of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/AQFT"} AQFT
          %p
            In this paragraph
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℳ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{M}
            will always be a von Neumann algebra acting on a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hilbert+space"} Hilbert space
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℋ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{H}
            with
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/commutant"} commutant
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ℳ
                    %mo ′
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{M}'
          %h3#vectors Vectors
          .un_defn
            %h6#definition Definition
            %p
              A
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/vector"} vector
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi x
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi ℋ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x \in \mathcal{H}
              is a
              %strong
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cyclic+vector"} cyclic vector
              if
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ℳ
                    %mi x
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{M}x
              is dense in
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi ℋ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{H}
          .un_defn
            %h6#definition_2 Definition
            %p
              A
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/vector"} vector
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi x
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi ℋ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x \in \mathcal{H}
              is a
              %strong
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/separating+vector"} separating vector
              if
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi M
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi x
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo =
                    %mn 0
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M(x) = 0
              implies
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi M
                    %mo =
                    %mn 0
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M = 0
              for all
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi M
                      %mo ∈
                      %mi ℳ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M \in \mathcal{M}
          .un_theorem
            %h6#theorem Theorem
            %p
              The notions of cyclic and separating are dual with respect to the commutant, that is a vector is cyclic for
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ℳ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{M}
              iff it is separating for
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi ℳ
                      %mo ′
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{M}'
          %h3#projections_in_von_neumann_algebras Projections in von Neumann algebras
          %p One crucial feature of von Neumann algebras is that they contain “every projection one could wish for”: there are three points that make this statement precise:
          %ul
            %li
              %p the linear combinations of projections are norm dense in a von Neumann algebra
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Gleason%27s+theorem"} Gleason's theorem
            %li
              %p
                Murray–von Neumann classification of
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/von+Neumann+algebra+factor"} factors
          %h4#projections_are_norm_dense Projections are norm dense
          %p
            First let us note that every element
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            of a von Neumann algebra can trivially be written as a linear combination of two selfadjoint elements:
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                  %mo =
                  %mfrac
                    %mn 1
                    %mn 2
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi A
                  %mo +
                  %msup
                    %mi A
                    %mo *
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo +
                  %mi i
                  %mfrac
                    %mn 1
                    %mrow
                      %mn 2
                      %mi i
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi A
                  %mo −
                  %msup
                    %mi A
                    %mo *
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  A = \frac{1}{2} (A + A^*) + i\frac{1}{2i} (A - A^*)
          %p
            Then, by the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spectral+theorem"} spectral theorem
            every selfadjoint element A is represented by it’s spectral measure E via
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                  %mo =
                  %msubsup
                    %mo ∫
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ‖
                      %mi A
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ‖
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ‖
                      %mi A
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ‖
                  %mi λ
                  %mi E
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi d
                  %mi λ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  A = \integral_{-\|A\|}^{\|A\|} \lambda E(d\lambda)
          %p The integral converges in norm to A and all spectral projections are elements of the von Neumann algebra. It immediatly follows that the set of finite sums of multiples of projections is norm dense in every von Neumann algebra.
          %h4#GleasonsTheorem Gleason’s theorem
          %p
            See
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Gleason%27s+theorem"} Gleason's theorem
          %h4#murrayvon_neumann_classification_of_factors Murray–von Neumann classification of factors
          %p To be done…
          %h3#miscellaneous Miscellaneous
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/split+inclusion+of+von+Neumann+algebras"} split inclusion of von Neumann algebras
          %h2#related_concepts Related concepts
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/enveloping+von+Neumann+algebra"} enveloping von Neumann algebra
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/von+Neumann+algebra+factor"} von Neumann algebra factor
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/W-star+category"} W-star category
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+relation"} quantum relation
          %h2#References References
          %p
            The original definition is due to
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/John+von+Neumann"} John von Neumann
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/J.+v.+Neumann"}> J. v. Neumann
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %em Zur Algebra der Funktionaloperationen und Theorie der normalen Operatoren
              = succeed "." do
                %a{:href => "http://dx.doi.org/10.1007/bf01782352"} doi
          %p Monographs:
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Masamichi+Takesaki"} Masamichi Takesaki
                = succeed "Theory" do
                  %em ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %strong 124
                = succeed "," do
                  %strong 125
                %strong 127
                (1979, 2002, 2003)
            %li#Blackadar06
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Bruce+Blackadar"}> Bruce Blackadar
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em
                    Operator Algebras – Theory of
                    = succeed "-Algebras" do
                      %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                        %semantics
                          %mrow
                            %msup
                              %mi C
                              %mo *
                          %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C^\ast
                = succeed "," do
                  %strong 122
                = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                  %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1007/3-540-28517-2"} doi:10.1007/3-540-28517-2
          %p
            On
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/von+Neumann+algebras"} von Neumann algebras
            in
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebraic+quantum+field+theory"} algebraic quantum field theory
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hans-J%C3%BCrgen+Borchers"}> Hans-Jürgen Borchers
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Jakob+Yngvason"} Jakob Yngvason
              = succeed "," do
                %em From quantum fields to local von Neumann algebras
              %strong 4
              spec01 (1992) 15-47 &amp;lbrack;
              %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1142/S0129055X92000145"}> doi:10.1142/S0129055X92000145
              \&amp;rbrack;
          %p See also:
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Jacob+Lurie"}> Jacob Lurie
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em von Neumann algebras
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "http://www.math.harvard.edu/~lurie/261y.html"} web
            %li
              %p
                Abraham Westerbaan,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em The Category of von Neumann Algebras
                %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/1804.02203"} 1804.02203
                2018
            %li#CK
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Alexandru+Chirvasitu"}> Alexandru Chirvasitu
                , Joanna Ko,
                %em
                  Monadic forgetful functors and (non-)presentability for C∗- and W
                  %em
                    \-algebras_,
                    = succeed "." do
                      %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/2203.12087"} arXiv:2203.12087
          %p
            In
            = succeed "/" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+physics"} quantum physics
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+computing"} quantum computing
          %p
            as
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/categorical+semantics"} categorical semantics
          %p
            for the
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+lambda-calculus"} quantum lambda-calculus
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Kenta+Cho"}> Kenta Cho
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Abraham+Westerbaan"} Abraham Westerbaan
              = succeed "," do
                %em Von Neumann Algebras form a Model for the Quantum Lambda Calculus
              = succeed "," do
